WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 3.3 · Structure, Governance & Accountability
Governance structures by legal form

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
You have governance from day one: decide who will direct and oversee, before you sign founding documents.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:28.133
Governance is how an organisation is directed and held to account. Management is how it is run day to day. You have some governance from day one, even if it is only how the founders decide.

4
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:29.133
Two jobs, often the same people

5
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:42.315
Governance: Asks: are we doing the right things, well and lawfully? Sets strategy, budget and key policies. For example: The two bakery founders meet quarterly to approve the budget and choose a legal form.

6
00:00:42.315 --> 00:00:54.015
Management: Asks: are we doing things right, day to day? Runs plans, schedules and delivery. For example: The same founders set this week's baking rota and book their kitchen slots.

7
00:00:54.015 --> 00:01:08.026
Advisory group: Adds expertise and voice, but does not decide unless your documents give it that role. For example: An accountant, a youth-centre manager and a shop owner advise the bakery twice a year.

8
00:01:08.426 --> 00:01:09.026
Who governs, by legal form

9
00:01:09.026 --> 00:01:15.811
If our bakery were a nonprofit: a board of trustees or directors, legally responsible.

10
00:01:15.811 --> 00:01:22.226
Company, our likely form: directors appointed by shareholders, who keep certain key decisions.

11
00:01:22.226 --> 00:01:30.752
If it were a cooperative: its members, who usually elect a committee to oversee the work.

12
00:01:31.152 --> 00:01:31.752
Why it matters early

13
00:01:31.752 --> 00:01:37.426
1. Your founding document fixes the rules, so decide before you sign.

14
00:01:37.426 --> 00:01:42.730
2. Clear roles stop founders confusing advice, oversight and daily decisions.

15
00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:49.033
3. Funders and partners trust organisations that are held to account.

16
00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:50.033
What it guides

17
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:55.707
1. Your choice of legal form and the founding document you adopt.

18
00:01:55.707 --> 00:02:01.011
2. Your reserved matters and governance calendar, in the next lesson.

19
00:02:01.011 --> 00:02:08.056
3. Who you invite first: advisers now, a fuller board as you grow.

20
00:02:08.456 --> 00:02:09.056
What to avoid

21
00:02:09.056 --> 00:02:14.730
Signing founding documents before deciding how you want to be governed.

22
00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:20.033
Treating an advisory group as if it carried legal responsibility.

23
00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:26.707
Skipping local rules: ask someone with a legal background before registering.

24
00:02:27.307 --> 00:02:29.107
You describe the governance your form requires and the one you want.

25
00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:33.107
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

26
00:02:34.607 --> 00:02:37.707
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
